  <article>
    <h3>Queensland Time Zone Policy</h3>
    <p>
      Queensland, home to Brisbane, Cairns, and the Gold Coast, observes Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST) all year. Due to the state's proximity to the equator, the northern regions experience very little daylight variation between summer and winter. While proposals are periodically debated in the state parliament, Queensland continues to reject daylight saving, remaining at UTC+10 while southern states shift to UTC+11.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>New South Wales and Sydney Time</h3>
    <p>
      New South Wales (NSW) follows AEST (UTC+10) during winter. On the first Sunday of October, clocks are advanced to AEDT (UTC+11) to extend evening daylight. The state reverts to AEST on the first Sunday of April. Broken Hill, situated on the western border, follows South Australian time regulations instead of NSW rules.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Victoria and Melbourne time</h3>
    <p>
      Victoria follows the same daylight saving cycle as New South Wales, switching between AEST (UTC+10) and AEDT (UTC+11). Melbourne is aligned with Sydney year-round, ensuring seamless interstate trade and communication.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Tasmania and ACT</h3>
    <p>
      Tasmania and the Australian Capital Territory (ACT) observe the standard daylight saving schedule, shifting from AEST in winter to AEDT in summer. This alignment ensures that Canberra remains in the same time zone as Sydney and Melbourne.
    </p>
  </article>
