<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES & CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Confusions and Pitfalls in GMT to AEST Calculations</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>AEST vs AEDT (Daylight Saving Shifts)</h3>
    <p>
      The most common error is assuming Sydney and Melbourne remain at UTC+10 all year. In summer, these states switch to AEDT (UTC+11). If you apply a +10 offset during these months, your meeting calendar will be off by one hour. Remember that Queensland (Brisbane) stays on AEST all year.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>GMT vs UTC Nomenclature</h3>
    <p>
      While practically identical, UTC is the modern atomic time scale, while GMT is the older astronomical standard. Software environments and database configurations should always use "UTC" or "Europe/London" rulesets rather than static "GMT" values to avoid scheduling issues.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Brisbane vs Sydney Discrepancies</h3>
    <p>
      During summer, Australia's Eastern coast is split: Brisbane uses AEST (UTC+10) while Sydney uses AEDT (UTC+11). If you schedule a call with participants in both cities, you must treat them as separate time zones. Do not assume all Eastern states are synchronized during the summer.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Western Australia (AWST) and Central Australia (ACST) Differences</h3>
    <p>
      Do not confuse AEST with other Australian offsets. Western Australia (AWST) is UTC+8 (2 hours behind AEST), and Central Australia (ACST) is UTC+9:30 (30 minutes behind AEST). Using the wrong offset will cause significant scheduling issues.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     REAL-WORLD USE CASES
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="use-cases" id="use-cases" aria-labelledby="usecases-heading">
  <h2 id="usecases-heading">Real-World Use Cases for GMT to AEST Conversion</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>Software Development and Server Logging</h3>
    <p>Database architectures log event timestamps in UTC (GMT) to maintain a consistent timeline across geographical regions. When rendering transaction ledgers, analytics dashboards, or system events to users in Sydney or Brisbane, developers must map the UTC timestamps back to AEST or AEDT. Using the correct IANA timezone file is essential for accurate calculations.</p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Financial Markets and Stocks Trading</h3>
    <p>The Australian Securities Exchange (ASX), located in Sydney, opens at 10:00 AM local time. UK and global traders need to monitor the ASX relative to GMT. Since Sydney uses AEDT in summer, the opening time shifts from 12:00 AM GMT (winter) to 11:00 PM GMT (summer). Having an accurate, real-time converter is critical for timely trade execution.</p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Esports and Online Gaming</h3>
    <p>International gaming tournaments coordinate scheduling across different continents using GMT/UTC. An announcement that a game lobby opens at 8:00 AM GMT requires Australian players to join at 6:00 PM AEST. Using a converter prevents players from missing their matches.</p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Travel Planning to Australia</h3>
    <p>Flights from Europe to Eastern Australia typically require 22 to 26 hours of travel. Adjusting for the 10-hour shift is essential for booking transport, confirming hotel arrivals, and scheduling meetings upon landing. Converting your travel itinerary into AEST helps you plan effectively.</p>
  </article>
</section>

